  "details": "<p>This site is related to, but distinct from, <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../894089\" target=\"_self\" title=\"Sippar: a Pleiades place resource\">Sippar</a>.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Sippar-Amnanum (modern Tell ed-Der; also referred to as Sippar-Annun\u012btu) was a small city located 6 km northeast of Sippar and 63 km north of Babylon. The goddess Annun\u012btu was the patron deity of this sister city of Sippar, and Eulma\u0161 was its principal temple. An archive of ca. 2,000 cuneiform tablets dating the the reigns of several kings of the First Dynasty of Babylon was excavated in the house of Ur-Utu, the chief lamentation-priest of the goddess Annun\u012btu. From several inscriptions of Nabonidus (r. 555\u2013539 BC), Babylon\u2019s last native king, certainly, Sippar-Amnanum (which he refers to as Sippar-Annun\u012btu) was still occupied and its cult was still in use in the sixth century BC, although it had been (partially) destroyed by the seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Sennacherib (r. 704\u2013681 BC).",
